This is a very common fear, but I can confidently tell you: Your fear is misplaced.
In your specific architecture (VaidyaVaani), AWS Lambda will likely be the CHEAPEST part of your monthly bill.
Here is the "Napkin Math" to prove it and put your mind at ease.
1. The Math: Why Lambda is Dirt Cheap
AWS Lambda pricing has two parts:
 * Per Request: $0.20 per 1,000,000 requests.
 * Duration: How long the code runs (milliseconds).
Scenario: You get 1 Million Emergency Calls.
 * Each call triggers Lambda ~5 times (Check user, Send to Bedrock, Book Ambulance, etc.).
 * Total Invocations: 5 Million Lambda runs.
 * Average run time: 200ms (It just hits an API and quits; it doesn't "listen" to the call).
The Bill:
 * Requests: 5M * ($0.20 / 1M) = $1.00
 * Compute Duration: ~ $2.50 (depending on RAM).
 * Total Lambda Bill: ~$3.50 (₹290) for handling ONE MILLION emergency calls.
Compare that to EC2:
To handle 1 million calls with spikes, you would need a cluster of Load Balanced EC2 servers running 24/7. That would cost you $100 - $300 per month even if no one calls.
2. Where is the REAL Cost? (Watch these instead)
If you want to be careful about money, stop looking at Lambda and look at these two:
 * Amazon Connect (The Telephony):
   * Cost: ~$0.018 per minute (varies by region).
   * Risk: If a user stays on the line for 30 minutes chatting with the AI, you pay for 30 mins.
   * Fix: Set a "Max Call Duration" (e.g., 10 mins) in your flow logic.
 * Amazon Bedrock (The Brain):
   * Cost: You pay per 1,000 input/output tokens.
   * Risk: If you send the entire conversation history to Claude every time the user speaks, the token count explodes.
   * Fix: Use a "Rolling Window" context (only send the last 3 turns of dialogue) to keep the payload small.
3. The "Free Tier" Safety Net
For the duration of the Hackathon (and your MVP), Lambda is effectively FREE.
 * AWS Free Tier: You get 1 Million Requests and 400,000 GB-seconds of compute every month forever.
 * You won't pay a single rupee for Lambda during development.
�� Verdict
Do not optimize for Lambda costs. It is negligible.
Optimize for Bedrock Tokens and Connect Minutes. That is where the scalability budget goes.
